const published = readPublishedState().events.get("event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
if (!published) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fuel-reserves-2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels");
const exportBanSlot = "slot-f2bf3132-9514-4edd-b581-b8e985b06c33";
const exportBanOccurrence = "occurrence-522c54dc9c72d34cd045251d0446d02f5d8b392096eb5a90f19f3a54dff3d584-e226f86d06a4bf16";
const jointStatementEvidence = "story-pin:b8768f5309bb2ddd21cd78ec5994d2da8cc5c4934c184abbcfa8a643caf9a0bd";
const exportBanPassage = "refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries";
const state = {
  ...published,
  current_state: Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(published.current_state ?? {}).filter(([, slot]) => slot.slot !== "early release window").map(([id, slot]) => id === exportBanSlot ? [id, { ...slot, quote_span: exportBanPassage }] : [id, slot])),
  occurrences: (published.occurrences ?? []).map((row) => row.id === exportBanOccurrence ? { ...row, evidence_id: jointStatementEvidence, quote_span: exportBanPassage } : row),
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fuel-reserves/">Fuel reserves</a><span>October 2, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Energy · October 2, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open data-layer="fact"><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
            <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
            <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
            <li><a href="#export-ban">The export ban</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What comes next</a></li>
            <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
            <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
          </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>
      <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 2, 2026, the leaders of the G7 countries agreed to release 100 million barrels of oil over four months, coordinated through the International Energy Agency, to begin immediately. Their statement promises a substantial diesel release within the first 20 days and gives no number for it. It sets that release "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Weekly U.S. retail diesel was {prices.dieselSep28} dollars a gallon on September 28. On March 2 it was {prices.dieselMar2}. The Independent says prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine. The G7 says the measures are meant to "shield households and businesses from price shocks", and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="shield households and businesses from price shocks" /><Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="American farmers, truckers, and businesses should not be left carrying the burden of a global diesel shortage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The European Commission's Energy Union task force said EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high.<Cite s="ec-taskforce-2026-10-02" passage="EU diesel supply remains stable for the time being, but prices remain high" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The French presidency's readout puts the figure as a ceiling: up to 100 million barrels within four months, notably of diesel and crude oil.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">In March, International Energy Agency members pledged {release.marchTotal} barrels; by October 2 around {marchSoFar.barrels} million of that March action had been released. The October statement does not say how much of its release is beyond those commitments. On September 30 President Donald Trump was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports. On October 1 the German government said buyers had drawn a first part of the release it authorized on March 18, and that the market did not consider a further release necessary. On October 2, hours after the agreement, he said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, and the joint statement commits the seven countries to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between themselves.<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`still "thinking about" banning diesel exports`} /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Deutschland hat die Freigabe am 18. März erteilt." /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Eine erste Tranche dieser für die Freigabe vorgesehenen Mengen wurde mittlerweile von den Marktteilnehmern abgerufen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Derzeit wird vom Markt keine weitere Freigabe für erforderlich gehalten." /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, hours after G7 countries agreed to release diesel and crude oil from their emergency reserves." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The International Energy Agency said in March that the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. The October decision also says the countries will coordinate maintenance schedules across their refineries to prevent simultaneous shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization where feasible, and it encourages engagement with countries that hold significant refining capacity to boost production of refined products, particularly diesel. The agency's September report says net exports of diesel and gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East, and that exports from the Gulf and Russia together were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because conflicts involving Russia and the Middle East have cut exports.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East`} /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The same statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and "temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible", and "We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel".<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /></p>
          <p>The same statement says the release takes into account "commitments that have already been fulfilled," asks the International Energy Agency to monitor the March 2026 commitments, and says the group will meet again "to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." It also says the members "reaffirm our commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries," and that a follow-up report "should be delivered before 20 days." It calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency published the same English statement, including the {release.barrels} barrel release over {release.months} and the diesel release "within the first 20 days."<Cite s="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On March 11, Chris Wright said 32 member nations agreed to a "coordinated release of 400 million barrels," and that the United States would "release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week," over "approximately 120 days." He said the United States had arranged to replace the draw with "approximately 200 million barrels within the next year."<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The March 11 G7 presidency communique says leaders welcomed the International Energy Agency announcement, prepared at the G7's initiative, "de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques", to release up to 400 million barrels of strategic reserves.<Cite s="elysee-g7-communique-2026-03-11" passage="de libérer jusqu’à 400 millions de barils de réserves stratégiques" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The agency's account of the same meeting lists President Donald Trump, Chancellor Friedrich Merz, Prime Minister Mark Carney, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni, President Ursula von der Leyen, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband, and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="The meeting was attended by President Donald Trump of the United States, Chancellor Friedrich Merz of Germany, Prime Minister Mark Carney of Canada, Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni of Italy, President Ursula von der Leyen of the European Commission, UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband and Japanese Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs Kazuya Endo." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Independent reported that Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband would stand in for Andy Burnham during the talks, as the Prime Minister attended his father's funeral.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband will stand in for Andy Burnham during urgent G7 talks regarding global energy markets, as the Prime Minister attends his father’s funeral." /></p>
          <p>The same article says fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="fuel prices hit £2 a litre for the first time" /></p>
          <p>It says the previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="The previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022" /></p>
          <p>The same article says prices have rocketed because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's ongoing war in Ukraine.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The French presidency's readout says the G7 leaders decided unanimously. It says a letter from the president of the Republic to the president of the European Commission concerned running refineries at full capacity, and that first measures had already been decided at European level.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Ils ont décidé, à l'unanimité :" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="la lettre que le Président de la République avait envoyée à la Présidente de la Commission européenne" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The International Energy Agency's March 19 contributions page describes the 11 March agreement as one "to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market". It says the overall release "will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It calls the action "the sixth time" members have acted together since the agency "was created in 1974," after actions "in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022," and says the war in the Middle East is creating "the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market." Executive Director Fatih Birol's remarks are named on that page.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="The war in the Middle East is creating the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-workbook-2026-03-19" passage="Total IEA,426.03075080054771" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
          <summary>English statement and French readout</summary>
          <DataTable columns={["Record", "Wording of the 100 million barrels"]} rows={statementRows} />
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>A massive amount of diesel, and no number for it</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. Reuters reported that the United States had demanded 100 million barrels of diesel from large European countries within 20 days, and that EU governments that day discussed a French proposal for 50 million barrels of diesel from Europe and 50 million barrels of crude oil from International Energy Agency members. The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". The joint statement gives no diesel number, names no country that will supply it, and reports nothing delivered.<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="The US had demanded large European countries including France and Germany release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European Union countries discussed a French proposal on Friday to release diesel stocks" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="wsj-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
              <QuoteCard speaker="Donald Trump" when="October 2, 2026" where="Truth Social, carried by Trump's Truth" label="Trump's Truth">
                <p>Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately.<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /></p>
              </QuoteCard>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Oil reserve decisions</strong><span>March to October 2026.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={chronology} />
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Diesel and crude</strong><span>Weekly U.S. No. 2 diesel, Europe Brent, and Cushing WTI.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Series", "Observation", "Dollars"]} rows={priceRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="gasdesw-2026-10-03" kind="computed" detail>
          <p>Diesel is {prices.dieselFromMarch} dollars a gallon, or {prices.dieselFromMarchPercent} percent, above March 2. Brent is {prices.brentFromFebruary} dollars a barrel above February 27. WTI is {prices.wtiFromFebruary} dollars above February 27.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-03-02,3.897" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,71.32" /><Cite s="dcoilwtico-2026-10-03" passage="2026-02-27,66.96" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How far prices have moved</strong><span>Diesel since September 21 and March 2. Brent and WTI since February 27.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Comparison", "Change", "Unit"]} rows={changeRows} />
        </figure>
        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 29 the Energy Department issued a request for an "exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, continuing its execution of the 172 million barrel release announced in March. Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. Secretary Chris Wright said several European member countries "have released only a fraction" of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged. The department said five earlier solicitations "collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels," with a "25 percent premium in returned barrels." Bids are due "October 6, 2026." Deliveries under those awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="participating companies will return the 40 million borrowed barrels with additional premium barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="several European member countries have released only a fraction of the crude oil and petroleum products they pledged" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bessent-post-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 1, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent wrote: "Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available."<Cite s="bessent-post-2026-10-01" passage="Our European partners should accelerate delivery on their existing commitments and make additional supplies immediately available" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" kind="record" detail>
          <p>U.S. stocks of distillate fuel oil were {distillate.sep25} thousand barrels in the week of September 25, down from {distillate.sep18} thousand the week before.<Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="Stocks of Distillate Fuel Oil" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="(Thousand Barrels)" /><Cite s="eia-distillate-2026-10-03" passage="08/21/26 08/28/26 09/04/26 09/11/26 09/18/26 09/25/26 View History U.S. 103,391 104,187 106,274 107,859 107,431 105,180" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The German government's energy FAQ says the 32 IEA members agreed in March to release "400 Millionen Barrel (gut 54 Millionen Tonnen)," and that "Deutschland hat die Freigabe am 18. März erteilt." It says Germany's emergency reserve "für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen," and that the IEA had asked Germany to release "2,6 Millionen Tonnen."<Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Deutschlands Notfallreserve für etwa 90 Tage umfasst insgesamt etwa 20 Millionen Tonnen" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Welt reported on Friday evening that the federal government welcomed the release of 100 million barrels of oil and products such as diesel.<Cite s="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" passage="Auch die Bundesregierung hat den G-7-Beschluss zur Freigabe von 100 Millionen Barrel Öl und Ölprodukten wie Diesel als wichtiges Zeichen für die Handlungsfähigkeit und Geschlossenheit der Gruppe begrüßt." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Interfax reported on September 30 that the Russian government extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel for producers until October 31.<Cite s="interfax-diesel-ban-2026-09-30" passage="The Russian government has extended the ban on exports of diesel fuel, ship fuel and gasoil for producers, which was set to expire today, until October 31" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On October 2 Reuters reported that Russia would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction.<Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="Russia said on Friday it would consider a partial lifting of diesel export restrictions in case of overproduction" /></p>
          <p>The same account says Deputy Prime Minister Alexander Novak said the domestic diesel market is currently balanced.<Cite s="reuters-novak-2026-10-02" passage="the domestic diesel market is currently balanced" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Reuters reported that the amount of oil in the reserve had sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="The amount of oil in US SPR has sunk to less than 284 million barrels, the lowest level since 1982." /></p>
          <p>The same article says US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="US law prohibits the president from ordering routine, small draw downs if the SPR falls below 252.4 million barrels." /></p>
          <p>It says an earlier offer drew agreement to borrow about 500,000 barrels.<Cite s="reuters-spr-limits-2026-09-29" passage="companies only agreed to borrow about 500,000 barrels" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>

      </section>

      <section id="export-ban">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The export ban</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">H.R. 10422, introduced September 16, would bar diesel exports after fourteen consecutive days above $5 a gallon, and "was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs".<Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="which was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">European Commission spokeswoman Anna-Kaisa Itkonen said, "We fully reject any ban on diesel."<Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="We fully reject any ban on diesel." /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Ursula von der Leyen, European Commission president" when="October 2, 2026" where="X post, via RTÉ" label="RTÉ">
          <p>We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks.<Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies" /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="We welcome the decision of G7 countries not to impose any export bans on allies and the continued solidarity between partners. We support an IEA-coordinated release of fuel stocks" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 16, 2026, Mr. Burchett and Mr. Fuller introduced H.R. 10422, "To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during certain periods." The bill tells the Secretary of Commerce to bar diesel exports while the average retail price stays above $5 a gallon for fourteen consecutive days, and to lift that bar after thirty consecutive days under $4.50. The text refers the bill to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10422-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="To prohibit the export of diesel fuel from the United States during" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The same day, the same sponsors introduced H.R. 10423. It says "the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited" from enactment through "December 31, 2026." It too was referred to the Committee on Foreign Affairs.<Cite s="hr10423-diesel-export-2026-09-16" passage="the export of diesel fuel shall be prohibited during the period beginning on the date of the enactment of this subsection and ending on the date December 31, 2026." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Politico reported that one European diplomat characterized the demand, set against the threat of a U.S. export ban, as blackmail.<Cite s="politico-diesel-pressure-2026-10-02" passage="One European diplomat characterized the White House’s demands for a release against the threat of a U.S. export ban explicitly as “blackmail,”" /><Cite s="reuters-diesel-demand-2026-10-01" passage="A second source, based in a European capital, said that the US has asked the EU to release 120 million barrels of diesel in the next six months." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Politico reported the president adding, "It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing".<Cite s="politico-no-ban-2026-10-02" passage="It was never really ever on the table, but what Europe did was a great thing" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The National reported that Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel.<Cite s="national-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="Mr Macron said Mr Trump did not threaten to issue an embargo on US diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" kind="record" detail>
          <p>An RBC note says several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban.<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="several leading Senate Republicans have publicly called for a diesel export ban" /></p>
          <p>The same note quotes the president on September 22: "I've said let's not send out the diesel."<Cite s="rbc-diesel-strategy-2026-10-01" passage="I've said let's not send out the diesel" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" kind="record" detail>
          <p>On September 23 Javier Blas wrote that industry groups had sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits on diesel exports.<Cite s="blas-export-letter-2026-09-23" passage="have sent a joint letter to President Trump urging him against limits of diesels exports." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Brownfield reported that Ohio is implementing a 90-day state gas and diesel tax suspension.<Cite s="brownfield-dyed-diesel-2026-10-02" passage="Ohio is implementing a 90-day state gas and diesel tax suspension." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Xinhua quoted the spokesperson: "It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner."<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="It would undermine our trust in the United States as a reliable partner." /></p>
          <p>The same account quotes the Commission president: "Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy."<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="Our citizens need and deserve affordable energy" /></p>
          <p>It says that since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled.<Cite s="xinhua-itkonen-2026-10-02" passage="since late February, gas prices in Europe have risen by 140 percent and diesel prices have doubled" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Scripps quoted Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins: "We are in the middle of harvest season."<Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="We are in the middle of harvest season" /><Cite s="daugherty-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="100 MILLION BARRELS OF DIESEL are currently en route to the United States, per Sec. Brooke Rollins" /></p>
          <p>She also said, "For the short term, we're going to do everything we can."<Cite s="scripps-rollins-2026-10-02" passage="For the short term, we're going to do everything we can" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What comes next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The G7 says it will meet again through the International Energy Agency in the coming days to discuss more diesel releases if needed. It invites the agency to monitor the impact of the measures and whether the commitments are carried out, and it says a follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will convene in the context of the IEA in the coming days to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We invite the IEA to monitor the impact of these measures on energy security and market stability and to monitor implementation of commitments. A follow-up report should be delivered before 20 days" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 29, before the G7 decision, the U.S. Energy Department offered an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, continuing the 172 million barrels the United States announced in March. Participating companies return the borrowed barrels later with extra barrels. Bids are due October 6. Five previous solicitations awarded more than {release.awarded} barrels across four completed exchanges, and deliveries under the four exchanges are scheduled for November and December.<Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="an exchange of up to 40 million barrels of crude oil" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="continuing DOE’s execution of the 172-million-barrel release previously announced by President Trump in coordination with the International Energy Agency (IEA) member nations’ overall 400-million-barrel commitment" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="25 percent premium in returned barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="October 6, 2026" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="This action builds on the Department’s five previous solicitations that collectively awarded more than 133 million barrels across four completed exchanges" /><Cite s="doe-spr-exchange-2026-09-29" passage="Deliveries under awarded exchanges are scheduled for November and December 2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6" data-absence>The G7 statement, the French readout and the International Energy Agency's account of the meeting do not say how much of the 100 million barrels is beyond the March pledge, how it divides between diesel and crude, which countries will supply it, or whether any of it has been delivered. The prices are from before the decision: the newest weekly diesel reading is September 28 and the newest Brent reading is September 29.<Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-29,113.96" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Reuters reported that Brent settled down 6 cents, at $102.25 a barrel.<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="Brent settled down 6 cents, or 0.06%, at $102.25 a barrel." /><Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="80 percent of overall IEA stocks “still in our pocket”" /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /></p>
          <p>The same story says WTI finished down $1.76, at $91.11 a barrel.<Cite s="reuters-settle-2026-10-02" passage="WTI finished down $1.76, or 1.90%, at $91.11 a barrel." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas wrote that the price of US wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel was back to the level of five weeks ago.<Cite s="blas-wholesale-2026-10-02" passage="The price of US wholesale ultra-low sulphur diesel is back to the level of five weeks ago." /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Javier Blas wrote that asking whether the release includes March barrels that were not released is a red herring, and that the question is whether more barrels reach the market now.<Cite s="blas-red-herring-2026-10-02" passage="It's a red herring: the important question is whether more barrels reach the market now" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

